Time for your Self-Report

Your answers help your case manager build a case plan that's right for you. A case plan is what helps get you into programs — and programs can help you lower your custody points, build a positive track record, and work toward your goals.

Without a self-report, your case manager can't build that plan. And if a program isn’t on your case plan, it may not count toward lowering your custody points, even if you complete it.

These questions should take around 15 minutes to finish.

A few things to know:

It's not just about your past. Many questions are about what you're doing in prison now — jobs, classes, staying out of trouble. Your answers help your case manager update their picture of who you are today.
Programs count more when they’re on your case plan: You could take the same program without a self-report, but it won't count toward case factor point reductions unless it's on your case plan first.
Your answers won't be used against you. This is not an admission of guilt. Only your case manager sees your answers to build your case plan.
Serving a long sentence? This still matters. A strong record of programs and jobs will help you build your case for a MAPP and can help move you to lower custody levels.
